title a guard that needs what no clone carries, a law the room that writes it did not keep, and a number already taken
prompt the standing baton -- take the next agent-doable lap in the auditing lane, reds first
rota lap N 4066, N mod 5 = 1, Air -- Saturn, law and boundary; threshold air-the-row-that-feels, then TAME_CORE, single-stranded, GAUGE_STYLE
think The air lap's own test is tactile -- press each boundary and see whether the hand passes through. TAME_CORE is the cardinal seat and it opens with an em dash, so I pressed the ASCII-first law first and the hand went straight through.
think My first measurement was wrong and the tree was right. I counted non-ASCII with a bash grep that this bench aliases to ugrep, which classifies a high-byte file binary and answers nothing with exit 1 -- byte-identical to a clean file. The scan I was auditing carries a comment warning of exactly that. Re-measured under sh and GNU grep, which is what the tree actually runs.
think Reds first preempted the keystone. The cold roster came back 119 guards, 115 green, 1 red, 778s, and the one red was sow_allow_reach -- seated by a peer at 12:26 today, seven minutes before my open. Its scan opens seed/, which is gitignored and built by sow.rish, so it refuses on every clone that has never projected. The refusal is right (%170); the roster row was what was wrong.
think The cost is what made it the crux rather than a note. A red guard sets roster_receipt_write=withheld_guard_red, --scoped refuses without a full green receipt, so every ship pays a full cold pass every lap -- eight ships against a measured 778s, from 12:26 forward.
think The roster already had the word. capability is a tier for what a host CAN DO, with three answers where absence alone skips and it must be positively read. I added a seed_projection arm probing test -d "${SOW_SEED:-seed}" -- the guard's own scan's question, read from the guard's own environment variable, so probe and guard cannot disagree about where the projection is.
think There is no unknown answer for this arm and I wrote that down rather than faking one. test -d has no tool that can go missing. The one thing absence could hide is a projection deleted where it should stand, and sow.rish rebuilds it from the field every publish, so a missing seed/ names no fault -- and the skip is announced by name on every pass.
think Then the keystone. ASCII-first names three subjects and I measured all three rather than assuming the gap. Comments have two meters over every tracked source. Commit bodies read 0 of the last 400, so the habit holds with no wall and I built none -- a wall where nothing is failing teaches nothing. Documents had living_card_ascii_scan: two pins hard, six advisory, eight files of 5,529.
think The gap sat where it cost most. 63 of the 104 rule pages an editor reads as law held 859 non-ASCII characters, and every single one is a form the rule's own substitution table spells -- 282 em dashes, 145 middle dots, arrows, ellipses, curly quotes, three comparison signs. Not one was a named exception.
think I measured whether the sweep would move rule_twin before running it, and the answer was no in both directions: normalising both twins with the substitution left zero pairs newly agreeing, so the twin drift is entirely substantive. Identical transform on both sides means drift can only hold or fall; it held at 39.
obs My own instrument failed the way this meter exists to catch, on its first run. The first draft put an awk comment between a trailing || and the newline continuing the statement; awk refused the whole program, the scan discarded the complaint with 2>/dev/null, and it answered a clean zero for all 5,529 documents. An empty answer from a failed read is byte-identical to an empty answer from a clean tree, and the second is the one everyone hopes for. Repaired by moving the table into a BEGIN array and checking awk's exit status at both call sites; planted as a control leg, where a chmod 000 file must make the scan say instrument=failed rather than count zero.
obs Sibling find, not taken: tools/fixtures/s/shell_comment_ascii_scan.sh and its Rye twin both run their per-file awk under 2>/dev/null with [ -z "$n" ] && n=0, which is the same swallow one room over. They are another seat's meters and they are green; naming it here rather than reaching for it.
obs The named/unnamed split is what makes the ratchet honest. 3,886 of the 3,956 characters are forms the rule spells and a program may convert; 70 are notation the table does not name -- a section sign, a Greek letter, superscripts, check marks -- and those carry a meaning a reader must choose the ASCII form for. The ceiling gates the total so the split cannot be gamed by reclassifying.
obs The sweep is proven rather than reviewed. --verify re-derives each page from its committed bytes through the same pure function and compares hashes: 104 checked, 104 proven. A hand edit beside the transform breaks it, which the control plants. Sixty-three files changed and no line reflowed, no word edited, no link retargeted.
obs The card had six bytes of slack. Both my findings are CLOSED and neither waits on Keaton's word, so by the card's own fold law the whole account went to a shelf and a pointer stayed. %491 folded to its own REDS shelf in the same pass to make room for %493.
obs The third finding came from grading my own work, which is the report card doing its job on the hand holding it. My REDS shelf read composite 59 -- below B -- and the reason was truth_counted=40, three of eight cited paths unresolved. All three were placeholder link shapes quoted inside backticks: `](X)`, `](../../Y)`, `](target)`. Markdown renders those as literal text, so they cite nothing and cannot be broken.
obs The tool had already written the gap down and declined to guess at it: "a link inside a fenced block or a backtick span reaches 20+ documents and wants its own round with its own measurement." That is a booking rather than a defect, so I ran the round with the measurement -- 30 wholly code-spanned link shapes across 12 tracked pages -- rather than booking a red for it.
obs The bitter half is who was being penalised. Every one of the 12 pages is a rule page, a design essay, or a REDS shelf teaching the fold rule, because stamp-and-name asks that an example path be built from placeholders. The reading took 20 points of Truth from exactly the pages that obeyed the law most carefully.
obs The repair runs one way and I proved that rather than asserting it. Masking can only remove candidate targets, so unresolved can only fall and a grade can only rise or hold; nothing that resolves today stops resolving. Both halves are planted anyway -- a genuinely broken link in plain prose still costs and is still named, and a line carrying a real link beside a quoted shape keeps the real one and cites once -- because a mask that also hid a real link would be a way to stop being checked.
obs The card had six bytes of slack when I arrived and my account would not fit at any honest length, so the whole account went to a shelf and a nine-line pointer stayed. That is the card's own fold law rather than a concession: both findings are CLOSED and neither waits on Keaton's word.
obs %493 had a second half and only the hot pass could find it. With sow_allow_reach skipped by capability it writes no run-card line -- which the capability control asserts on purpose -- so the red row from the cold pass was carried forward by the runner's own history rule and nothing would ever overwrite it. The roster's own guard counts card reds, so a guard rescued from redding every tree would have red every tree forever by a different road.
obs The carry-forward exists so a tier cadence guard keeps its history between runs, and that is right. It is wrong for a guard skipped by host or capability: that guard cannot run here at all, so its last verdict was recorded in a different world. Those rows are dropped now and a tier-cadence row is still kept, both planted, the drop planted as a stale RED because that is the direction that costs.
obs Two hot passes, and the first one's reds were mine to have foreseen: booking a REDS row moves the ledger's headline count, and reds_ledger_monotone and reds_ledger_headline both read it. One writer, tools/fixtures/r/reds_ledger_headline_write.sh, fixed both -- and my hand edit of the same line first was the wrong move, since the writer also carries a span the headline recites.
obs The rebase brought four reds and three were repairs a promise owed. The front door linked GLOW_HOST.template.bron and GLOW_PROFILE.template.bron, both renamed to .kyri by an upstream commit minutes old, so README named two files that do not exist -- and that one break red BOTH seed_link and readme_reach, since a shipped page linking outside the seed and a living page holding a broken link are the same two links read twice. The card also linked rows-492 for a shelf named rows-494. Repaired, and named rather than booked: a peer's commit minutes old, and this day already carries five duplicated bookings.
obs The fourth was %440 firing again, on my own rebase, exactly where the last two laps said it would. Upstream's 124340 row auto-merged above my 133800 one and broke newest-first; one sort by stamp descending moved it back and index_row_bound read ok. That is its twelfth firing across the fleet by my count, and the ninth I have watched land on a shelf after a rebase rather than before it.
