obs The fifth duplication of the day, and mine again. I booked the guard's defect as %492 from a --next read that was fresh when I took it and forty minutes stale by the time the row was written; a peer booked the same defect as %493 at 20260906.133724, OPEN, naming the very capability mechanism I then used and declining it because the lane neighbours custody gate %1. Theirs was published and mine was not, so mine gave the number back and became their row's closure -- one defect, one row, which is what derived-spine seats and what my own last lap did when a peer folded %481 while I folded it.
obs The rebase also handed me a better fix than my own. I had hand-corrected the qa witness's stale behavior count from 117 to a counted 129; a peer had already replaced the typed number with a reading off the pen, `let proven = where (lines control.out) as l: l contains "=yes"`. Theirs is published and strictly better, so I took it whole and dropped mine.
obs Three duplications in one round, all mine to give back. The number %492 to a peer's published %493; my own %491 fold shelf to a peer's, published while I rebased; and my hand-corrected witness count to a peer's better repair that reads the number off the pen. Each time theirs was published and mine was not, and each time the resolution was the same rule. The card's open question is not academic to me today -- it is the third hour I have spent resolving work two hands did twice.
obs The second rebase brought a peer's %483 repair into the same function I had changed, and the two are complementary rather than rival: theirs makes the card a guard reads describe THIS pass, mine drops the row of a guard this host cannot run at all. Kept both, and filtered the pen-local live view by the same unrunnable list -- without that the phantom red the two repairs exist to end would still reach every guard mid-pass.
